channel.item:
      Web Assembly, Python, and the Input Challenge
      http://localhost:1313/post/wasm-python-input-issues/
      Tue, 10 Oct 2023 10:06:07 -0500

      http://localhost:1313/post/wasm-python-input-issues/
      <p class="post-p">One of the challenges in translating Python to the web is this very simple piece of example code, pulled <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#if-statements">straight from the Python docs:</a></p>
<div class="highlight"><div style="background-color:#f0f3f3;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">
<table style="border-spacing:0;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;"><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;">
<pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f0f3f3;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">1
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">2
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">3
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">4
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">5
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">6
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">7
</span><span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f">8
</span></code></pre></td>
<td style="vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;;width:100%">
<pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f0f3f3;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;"><code class="language-python3" data-lang="python3"><span style="display:flex;"><span>x <span style="color:#555">=</span> <span style="color:#366">int</span>(<span style="color:#366">input</span>(<span style="color:#c30">&#34;Please enter an integer: &#34;</span>))
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#069;font-weight:bold">if</span> x <span style="color:#555">&lt;</span> <span style="color:#f60">0</span>:
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>    x <span style="color:#555">=</span> <span style="color:#f60">0</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>    <span style="color:#366">print</span>(<span style="color:#c30">&#39;Negative changed to zero&#39;</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#069;font-weight:bold">elif</span> x <span style="color:#555">==</span> <span style="color:#f60">0</span>:
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>    <span style="color:#366">print</span>(<span style="color:#c30">&#39;Zero&#39;</span>)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span><span style="color:#555">...</span></span></span></code></pre></td></tr></table>
</div>
</div>

<p class="post-p">The problem is with the <code class="code">input</code> statement, which essentially tells the Python interpreter's thread to block (i.e. lock up, freeze, do nothing) until it receives data from an external source (the terminal) that it can respond it.</p>
<p class="post-p">Unfortunately, this doesn't translate well to the web. The idea of 'block the interpreter thread until something else wakes it up' is that, in the Browser, there is only one thread*! Hence, there's no 'something else' to come along and wake the interpreter's thread back up. Nor is there any equivalent of "ctrl+c", no way to "kill" that thread from within the browser - something would have to be listening for that singal and reach out and kill the thread with the interpreter... and again, there's only one thread.</p>


